# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
more_disgraceful	one
more_painful	two	pain	P4	exceeds in the pain by which disgrace is measured, i.e. causes more pain overall, to anyone affected or looking on	the doers themselves suffer more pain than the victims
more_evil	one
# more_painful: In S11 to S15, disgrace is measured by the pain a thing causes, which by analogy with beauty pleasing its spectators need not be the agent's own pain. S22 to S24 test only whether the injurers suffer more than the injured. So doing wrong could cause more pain in the first sense even though it fails in the second.
# more_evil: I was unsure here. S36's "more evil" may be heard as worse for the doer, set against Polus's S18 sense of worse for whoever suffers it. But the listed statements S20 and S32 use the one standard sense of evil as harm.
